WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.660
With a sneaky villainous twist and a cliffhanger
ending to set up Season 2, Jenna Ortega's

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.760
gloriously deadpan take on Wednesday Addams
is plenty of fun!

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.050
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan, and
in this video I'm breaking down the ending

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:20.720
of Wednesday Season 1 and revealing the hidden
clues to the mystery that even Wednesday herself

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:21.720
missed.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.779
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:28.350
The puppet master at the heart of the first
season's whodunit is Miss Thornhill, Nevermore's

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:33.680
resident dorm mum and botany teacher, whose
real name is actually Laurel Gates.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.879
Although Wednesday doesn't realise that Thornhill's
the secret villain until Eugene says he saw

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:43.480
red boots on the person who set the cave on
fire, as viewers we should have been suspicious

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.320
of her when she introduced herself for the
first time.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.660
Good evening, girls.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.270
Oh, sorry about the mud.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.239
In the scene shortly before this, we'd seen
the sheriff and his deputy in the woods in

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:57.450
the rain, surveying the crime scene of the
hiker killed by the monster.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:03.120
So, when Miss Thornhill stamps her muddy boots
while claiming she's only been in her conservatory,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.689
it's a clue that she'd likely been outside
in the woods near the murder scene instead.

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:12.740
All of Thornhill's connections with dangerous
plants were hinted at right in the opening

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.729
episode when we saw her smiling as she fed
live worms to some Venus fly traps.

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:23.311
Wednesday later deduces that after Thornhill
revealed to Tyler the family secret that his

00:01:23.311 --> 00:01:29.009
own mother was a Hyde, she used extracts from
her plants to fully unlock the Hyde inside

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:30.009
him.

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:33.990
But well before Wednesday figured that out,
there were several clues to Miss Thornhill's

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.680
interest in botanical chemical trickery.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:42.930
Most plants reward their pollinators with
sweet nectar, many carnivorous varieties turn

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.439
to sexual trickery or deception.

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:49.969
And the way that Hydes effectively become
slaves to those who unlock their power…

00:01:49.969 --> 00:01:53.210
This act causes the Hyde to develop an immediate
bond with its liberator, who the creature

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:54.499
now sees as its master.

00:01:54.499 --> 00:01:59.170
… was also hinted at when Thornhill explained
another trick from the plant world.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:05.640
The orchid produces a pheromone that mimics
a female insect, luring the males in.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.390
Thornhill even gives herself away a little
right at the very end, when just before Wednesday's

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:15.110
expelled, she gives her an oleander plant
as a parting gift, which as Wednesday observed

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.280
is especially toxic.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.280
White oleander.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.990
One of nature's deadliest.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:23.840
The body parts that Thornhill collects and
stores in jars are part of her plan to resurrect

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.190
her ancestor and original zealot Joseph Crackstone.

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:31.260
And there's a little clue to this earlier
on when she presents Wednesday with a copy

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.900
of the classic novel Frankenstein by Mary
Shelley.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.590
Similar to how Thornhill brings to life the
monstrous Crackstone with a spell that conducts

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:44.590
electricity between the body parts she'd been
collecting, in Shelley's novel Victor Frankenstein's

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:48.260
monster was created from a collection of animal
and human pieces.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:53.840
And the idea of electricity bringing Frankenstein's
creature to life became popularised after

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.310
the 1931 movie.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:00.560
When Crackstone is mentioned for the first
time as Jericho's founding father, a couple

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.870
of banners for Pilgrim World are unveiled
with the words "Where History Comes To Life"

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.629
together with a picture of the long-dead pilgrim,
in a clever bit of foreshadowing and a hint

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:12.390
to his resurrection in the final episode.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:16.780
And just after Wednesday breaks into the old
meeting house and comes across a model of

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.260
the evil pilgrim, she narrates…

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.599
Secrets are like zombies…
they never truly die.

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:26.390
… which is another nice bit of foreshadowing
for how Crackstone can and will come back

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:29.290
as a kind living dead creature himself.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.790
And Crackstone's death at Wednesday's hands,
which sees him burning up into a cloud of

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:39.640
hot ash and smoke, was also telegraphed earlier
in the season when she and Thing set the memorial

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.750
statue of him on fire and the metal melted
into a black goo.

